</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">omralon@amazon.com</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We motivate and share a new benchmark<span id="footnotex4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        Benchmark URL forthcoming</span></span></span> for instance-level <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">performance prediction</span> of long-form generation tasks having multi-faceted, fine-grained quality metrics. Our task-, model- and metric-agnostic formulation predicts continuous evaluation metric scores given only black-box model inputs and outputs. Beyond predicting point estimates of metric scores, the benchmark also requires inferring prediction intervals to quantify uncertainty around point estimates. Evaluation spans 11 long-form datasets/tasks with multiple LLMs, baselines, and metrics per task. We show that scores can be effectively predicted across long-form generation tasks using as few as 16 training examples Overall, we introduce a novel and useful task, a valuable benchmark to drive progress, and baselines ready for practical adoption today.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:455.2pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;"><span id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
</span></span></p>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Both authors contributed equally.</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">**</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">**</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Work does not relate to the author’s position at Amazon.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) can tackle increasingly challenging open-ended, long-form generation tasks across diverse modalities (e.g., text, images, code, etc.) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Given the vast output space, many different generations of varying quality are possible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giulianelli et al. (2023)</a></cite>. This motivates evaluation of long-form output quality that is: 1) fine-grained and nuanced (rather than binary correct/incorrect), and 2) multi-faceted, with diverse evaluation metrics used to assess different aspects of output quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2025)</a></cite>. For example, summarization quality might be measured via aspects such as brevity, fluency, coherence, consistency, and relevancy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Like other AI models, LLMs are fallible, with varying output quality across different inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schellaert et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Consequently, the ability to predict the quality of each output – across different inputs, tasks, models, and metrics – would have myriad benefits. Key among these is to
guide appropriate trust and use for both human users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhuliawala et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2021)</a></cite> and downstream agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mielke et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoffe et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
For example, outputs predicted to be of high quality might be used as-is, while those of lesser quality might be revised or discarded accordingly. Predicted quality could also provide a signal to optimize prompt selection or model parameterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2022)</a></cite>, to select between different generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023)</a></cite>, or to route inputs to whichever model is predicted to perform best <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Šakota et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shnitzer et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. ()</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming evaluation metrics for fine-grained output quality are continuous <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we not only want to predict <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">point estimates</span> for metric scores, but also <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prediction intervals</span> to quantify uncertainty around point estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shrestha and Solomatine (2006)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patel (1989)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geisser (1993)</a></cite>. For example, given a point estimate prediction of 0.8, a tight 95% interval (say [0.79, 0.81]) would predict that output quality is quite close to the point estimate, while a loose 95% interval (say [0.6, 0.1]) would mean that quality may actually be much lower. Prediction intervals thus provide crucial uncertainty quantification for risk-averse settings by indicating probabilistic lower bounds on expected performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Harrington et al. (2019)</a></cite>.
We thus emphasize the importance of prediction intervals as well as point estimates for performance prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we motivate and formulate the task of <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">instance-level performance prediction</span> for long-form generation tasks: predicting instance-level output quality – point estimates and prediction intervals – for continuous evaluation metrics. By design, our task formulation is task-, model-, and metric-agnostic: given an input-output pair for any black-box model, one must predict arbitrary evaluation metrics. The generality of this formulation supports broad use across the diverse, ever-changing landscape of LLM models and evaluation metrics for long-form output quality. We assume supervision is key: by observing examples of past input-output pairs and corresponding metric scores, one can learn a metric-specific prediction function. An ideal solution would achieve strong predictive performance with minimal supervision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To promote research on instance-level performance prediction for long-form generation tasks, we create and share a new benchmark spanning twelve long-form tasks/datasets: text summarization, machine translation, code generation, and question answering. With different metrics often used to assess different aspects of output quality, our benchmark includes multiple evaluation metrics for most tasks. In regard to LLM coverage
, we include outputs from Llama 3.2
;other LLMs could be easily added later.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our benchmark includes two baselines. One uses off-the-shelf confidence estimators (verbalized confidence and output consistency) to elicit weak features, then trains a classic regression model (e.g., linear regression, random forests) to predict the metric score from the features. The other baseline uses a <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reference-free</span> LLM-as-a-Judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024a)</a></cite> with in-context learning for textual regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vacareanu et al. (2024)</a></cite> to directly predict the metric score from the input-output pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our work demonstrates the feasibility of instance-level prediction of continuous evaluation metric scores and associated uncertainty intervals. We show that scores can be predicted with low RMSE and CRPS, across long-form generation tasks, and using as few as 16 training examples (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Evaluating Performance Prediction ‣ 5 Benchmark ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>). While the feature-based regression baseline described above is shown to perform well, the LLM judge baseline proves inconsistent. We also see that some confidence-based features are much more predictive than others. Taken together, our formulation, benchmark, and findings provide both a strong foundation for future research as well as baselines suitable for practical use today.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our formulation of <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">performance prediction</span> draws upon a large body of prior work across related areas: <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Confidence Estimation</span> and <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Uncertainty Quantification</span> (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Uncertainty and Confidence Estimation ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), <span id="S2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Score and Benchmark Predictability</span> (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Score &amp; Benchmark Predictability ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), <span id="S2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Query Performance Prediction</span> in information retrieval (Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 QPP in Information Retrieval ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>), <span id="S2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quality &amp; Metric Estimation</span> in machine translation (Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Quality and Metric Estimation in MT ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>), <span id="S2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Textual Regression</span> (Section  <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Textual Regression ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>), and <span id="S2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-as-a-judge</span> &amp; <span id="S2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reward Models</span> (Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 LLM-as-a-Judge &amp; Reward Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Uncertainty and Confidence Estimation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work is particularly motivated by <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidence estimation</span> (CE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2024)</a></cite> and <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uncertainty quantification</span> (UQ) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vashurin et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2022)</a></cite>, which similarly predict how much each system output can be trusted. While terminology and definitions vary across different works, UQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith, 2013</a>)</cite> canonically seeks to quantify certainty regarding a given process, observation, or system output, while CE seeks to predict the probability that a system output is correct or not <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024)</a></cite> write, “the correctness of long-form generation is
not always either true or false but can be partially correct. Therefore, a single confidence score for long-form outputs is ambiguous: it can either imply ‘I am x% confident that the answer is 100% correct’ or ’I am 100% confident that the answer is x% correct.” Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ulmer et al. (2024)</a></cite> suggest estimation of LLM confidence intervals as future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, prior work has typically modeled long-form generation confidence via binary correctness by: i) mapping generations to a most similar multiple choice answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tjuatja et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, ii) binarizing partial-credit scores via thresholding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuhn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Band et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pedapati et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, iii) testing whether or not the output contains an answer (string) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajpurkar et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, iv) focusing on tasks with short answers wherein evaluation can rely on exact matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pedapati et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>; or v) decomposing a long output into smaller nuggets checked individually for correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In recent work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite> usefully infer partial-credit scores for
correctness at various granularities (e.g. sentence or passage level) but still assume that output quality can be measured via a single dimension of correctness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024)</a></cite> also emphasize “…the multifaceted nature of evaluating long-form outputs, where factors like factuality, coherence, clarity, and comprehensiveness each play a role…” However, they assume evaluation dimensions are implicit and latent, leading to subjective disagreements between evaluators. In contrast, we assume distinct evaluation metrics assess different aspects of output quality, and predict each separately. Prior studies also tend to show only correlation between CE vs. evaluation metric scores, while we predict metric scores directly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our formulation pursues uncertainty quantification via prediction intervals that quantify uncertainty around point estimates of metric scores.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Score &amp; Benchmark Predictability</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Like CE and UQ above, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">score predictability</span> work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernández-Orallo et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pacchiardi et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schellaert et al. (2025)</a></cite> similarly makes instance-level predictions of binary output correctness. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernández-Orallo et al.</a></cite> note continuous prediction is more complex and proceed with binary. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schellaert et al.</a></cite>  suggest future non-binary work while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pacchiardi et al.</a></cite> assume the binary case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Three other differences are noteworthy. First, score predictability emphasizes <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">anticipatory</span> prediction of instance-level success before output generation. This avoids computing outputs but makes prediction more difficult (without need for our use cases). Our performance prediction task more closely resembles UQ and CE in which confidence is inferred for a given output. Second, model details are often exploited to improve prediction, vs. our model-agnostic, black-box formulation. Third, score predictability methods tend to leverage a large feature set (e.g. text embeddings); we require only (input, output, score) triples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Other work on <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">benchmark prediction</span> infers continuous scores, but only for entire datasets in aggregate <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>. Instead, we predict the quality of individual outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>QPP in Information Retrieval</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our nomenclature of “performance prediction” is inspired by <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">query performance prediction</span> (QPP) work in information retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cronen-Townsend et al., 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">He and Ounis, 2006</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arabzadeh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Given an input query, QPP seeks to predict the quality of output search results. Like our work, QPP is both instance-based and predicts arbitrary, continuous-valued metrics of output quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">QPP work is split into <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pre-retrieval</span> vs. <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">post-retrieval</span> methods. Like <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">anticipatory</span> score predictability (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Score &amp; Benchmark Predictability ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), pre-retrieval methods require predicting output quality (of search results) before executing the (input) query. In contrast, post-retrieval methods run the query and analyze output search results to better predict their quality, and this helps them to perform much better in practice. We also expect output analysis to help prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Quality and Metric Estimation in MT</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Quality estimation</span> (QE) in machine translation (MT) predicts translation output quality at run-time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Specia et al. (2009)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Specia et al. (2010)</a></cite>. Earlier work on CE for MT also assumes binary correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blatz et al. (2004)</a></cite> which QE then generalizes to continuous, partial-credit evaluation. The term CE is also still sometimes used to refer to the broader QE task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chelba et al. (2020)</a></cite>. QE is canonically framed as a regression task to predict human quality ratings (i.e., “direct assessment”) of output quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Scarton et al. (2016)</a></cite> frame QE more broadly as predicting output quality across diverse NLP tasks, including text summarization and simplification as well as QE.
Some QE work has also emphasized the value of inferring prediction intervals beyond simple point estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glushkova et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giovannotti (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zerva and Martins (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zouhar et al. (2023)</a></cite> propose “metric estimation”: predicting evaluation metric scores rather than human quality ratings. However, their goal in doing so is to pre-train a QE model. We are agnostic as to the type of prediction target (i.e., metric score or human rating) used to evaluate long-form outputs. To illustrate this, we also include a “direct assessment” task in our evaluation (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Benchmark ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>), complementing our other metric prediction results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Textual Regression</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2022)</a></cite> note “almost no work in NLP on calibration in a regression setting” and consider three regression tasks: semantic similarity, sentiment analysis, and QE. They use BERT/SBERT and report point estimates and prediction intervals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-based work has pursue three primary approaches: i) extracting LLM features for classic regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, ii) leveraging in-context learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vacareanu et al. (2024)</a></cite>; and iii) fine-tuning for specific regression tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vacareanu et al. (2024)</a></cite> consider three synthetic regression tasks and show effective results across several models via in-context learning, but without prediction intervals. We report a similar in-context LLM baseline in this work (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>). Most recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vedula et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose quantile regression for LLM-based price estimation, reporting prediction intervals as well as point estimates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>LLM-as-a-Judge &amp; Reward Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rise of LLMs has also brought the advent of <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-as-a-judge</span>. A key distinction exists between <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reference-free (RF) methods</span>, suitable for predicting output quality at run-time, vs. <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reference-based (RB) methods</span> that require human reference outputs to compare AI outputs against <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al. (2024)</a></cite> present an excellent discussion relating RF methods to <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reward models</span> used in reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), noting “these two fields are closely related and are gradually converging.” Our work is related to both fields, though we focus on run-time prediction of RB metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also note similarity between RF LLM judges and “verbalized” CE methods (Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Uncertainty and Confidence Estimation ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) in which an LLM is prompted for its confidence that a given output is correct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Both support run-time use of LLMs through prompting, though verbalized CE methods have assumed a particular type of assessment (i.e., confidence) while RF LLM judges have been conceived and pursued much more broadly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While RF LLM judges are increasingly being used as final evaluators, their reliability remains contested: they can exhibit self<span id="S2.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>preference and positional biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, mis-rank instances they cannot themselves solve without a human reference answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krumdick et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and underperform or distort correctness in domains like software engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2025)</a></cite>. More broadly, RF methods have inherent structural biases—being effectively one generation model evaluating another—which risks test-time gaming, favoring models similar to the judge, and even disadvantaging higher-quality (including human) outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deutsch et al. (2021)</a></cite>. Reproducibility can also be limited by underlying changes in closed LLM judges or prompt sensitivity in use of open judges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bavaresco et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Meta-evaluation of RF methods often reports correlation with human judgments, but caution is warranted. For example, the breadth and diversity of correlation studies is limited <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bavaresco et al. (2025)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krumdick et al. (2025)</a></cite> note correlation can also mask disagreement on difficult instances, limiting ability to evaluate more advanced models or reliably predict instance-level quality. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dorner et al. (2025)</a></cite> show that when a RF LLM judge has comparable accuracy to an evaluated model, “no debiasing method can decrease the required amount of ground truth labels by more than half… [showing] severe limitations of the [RF] LLM-as-a-judge paradigm…" Their empirical results show practical performance is even worse than this theoretical limit. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gera et al. (2025)</a></cite> similarly report, “Crucially, even very good instance-level capabilities do not guarantee accurate model ranking.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While we expect RF LLM judges to both continue to improve and become better understood, the limitations above suggest that RB LLM judges (and perhaps classic RB metrics) still remain an important part of the NLP evaluation toolkit for evaluating long-form outputs. This motivates a continuing need for run-time prediction of RB evaluation metrics, as we pursue in this study.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Task Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prediction intervals represent a natural extension of uncertainty for point estimates, bounding the range within which the true performance score will fall with a specified probability (e.g. 95 %) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shrestha and Solomatine (2006)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patel (1989)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geisser (1993)</a></cite>. Prediction intervals are increasingly being reported, e.g., in MT quality estimation (Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Quality and Metric Estimation in MT ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) and other textual regression tasks (Section <a href="#S2.SS5" title="2.5 Textual Regression ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formalization.</span>

Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input space (user queries, upstream agent inputs) and <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> the long-form output space (e.g., text, code, etc.) for both LLM generations and gold reference outputs. We assume each task <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> continuous, reference-based (RB) evaluation metrics <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}=\{m_{t,1},\dots,m_{t,K_{t}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}=\{m_{t,1},\dots,m_{t,K_{t}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each metric
<math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t,k}:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{G}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t,k}:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{G}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
assigns LLM output <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> a real-valued quality score based on some measure of agreement with reference gold output <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. At inference, only input <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and generation <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are observed; gold output <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and metric score <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t,k}(y,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t,k}(y,g)</annotation></semantics></math> are not.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instance-level performance prediction.</span>
For each task-specific metric <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we seek a predictor</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t,k}:(\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y})\rightarrow\big(\hat{s},[\ell,u]\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t,k}:(\mathcal{X}\!\times\!\mathcal{Y})\rightarrow\big(\hat{s},[\ell,u]\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for instance <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> that returns: (i) a point estimate <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for metric score <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t,k}(y,g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t,k}(y,g)</annotation></semantics></math> and (ii) a <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> prediction interval <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\ell,u]\subseteq\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\ell,u]\subseteq\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\!\big(m_{t,k}(y,g)\in[\ell,u]\big)\geq 1-\alpha," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.090em;" width="1.090em"><mi>Pr</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\!\big(m_{t,k}(y,g)\in[\ell,u]\big)\geq 1-\alpha,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">for a specified confidence level (e.g., <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervision.</span>
For each task <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, assume we have a dataset <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> training instances <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x_{i},y_{i},s_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x_{i},y_{i},s_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each of <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>’s <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> metrics,
where <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=m_{t,k}(y_{i},g_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=m_{t,k}(y_{i},g_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes score <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for metric <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, as calculated for generation <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to gold reference <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. This setup is both metric-agnostic (allows any metric) and model-agnostic (makes no use of model parameters or decoding internals).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective:</span>
minimize point-estimation error and also produce calibrated intervals (see Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Evaluating Performance Prediction ‣ 5 Benchmark ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07309v1/images/illustration_of_conf_intervals.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/185;" width="628" height="185" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrating different regression model predictions (solid lines) and confidence intervals (color shading around the lines). In this example, a Bayesian Ridge regression model performs best as predictions are neither over-confident (XGBoost) nor under-confident (Gaussian Process).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Baseline Methods</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having defined the desired performance predictor <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Task Formulation ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we now describe two baseline methods for instantiating such a predictor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confidence-based regression (CE-Reg)</span>: Extract unsupervised confidence estimates as features, then learn a regression model to map features to the continuous metric score and a prediction interval. Methods are presented in Sections <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Confidence Estimation (CE) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Confidence-Based Regression (CE-Reg) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reference-Free LLM-as-a-Judge</span>: Prompt an LLM to directly output a point estimate and prediction interval for the target metric, using labeled examples for in-context learning. See Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Confidence Estimation (CE)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verbalized confidence (1S and 2S)</span>: Prompting an LLM for its confidence that an output is correct (see Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 LLM-as-a-Judge &amp; Reward Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). One-step (1S) produces both output and estimated confidence in one invocation, while two-step (2S) first generates the output and then calls the LLM a second time to estimate output confidence. While 1S reduces computation, 2S provides cleaner delineation for benchmarking generation vs. confidence estimation. Note that while 2S permits different LLMs to be used for each step, our experiments use the same LLM for both steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Consistency-based</span>: We generate <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> outputs and measure coherence between them via 5 methods: Degree Matrix (Deg; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite>), Eccentricity (Ecc; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite>), Sum of Eigenvalues of the Graph Laplacian (EigV; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite>), Lexical Similarity (LexSim; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fomicheva et al. (2020)</a></cite>), and the Number of Semantic Sets (NumSet; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2024)</a></cite>). Most use natural language inference (NLI) models to assess coherence between outputs, while LexSim is computed using ROUGE-L.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Confidence-Based Regression (CE-Reg)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a dataset
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(c_{i},s_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(c_{i},s_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N},</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> training examples, where each <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the confidence feature (verbalized or consistency-based) for output <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=m_{t,k}(y_{i},g_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=m_{t,k}(y_{i},g_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the gold score, we fit an auxiliary regression model to learn the conditional distribution
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\sim p(s\mid c_{i},\theta)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\sim p(s\mid c_{i},\theta),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=m_{t,k}(y_{i},g_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=m_{t,k}(y_{i},g_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the gold metric score. From the fitted distribution we extract a point estimate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> prediction interval <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\,\ell_{i},u_{i}\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\,\ell_{i},u_{i}\,]</annotation></semantics></math> (using <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) via</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}=F^{-1}\bigl(\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\bigr),\quad u_{i}=F^{-1}\bigl(1-\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}=F^{-1}\bigl(\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\bigr),\quad u_{i}=F^{-1}\bigl(1-\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s predictive cumulative distribution function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regression Models.</span> We compare a variety of models: linear (Linear, Bayesian Ridge), tree ensembles (Random Forest, XGBoost), and a Beta regression for bounded metrics. Gaussian-error models provide mean <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> &amp; variance <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> directly; ensembles approximate variance via bootstraps/trees.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<p id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.T1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:70%;width:455.2pt;">


<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t ltx_colspan ltx_colspan_2" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t ltx_colspan ltx_colspan_5" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CE<math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>Reg: Consistency-based</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t ltx_colspan ltx_colspan_2" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CE<math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>Reg: Verbalized</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t ltx_colspan ltx_colspan_3" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RF-LLMaaJ</span></span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DegMat.</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Eccen.</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EigVal.</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LexSim.</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NumSet.</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.8.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:34.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1S</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.9.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:34.1pt;">
<span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.2.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2S</span></span>
