<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">CRPS Benchmark.<span id="S4.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We compare two baselines: 1) confidence-based regression (CE-Reg) uses 7 CE features (five consistency-based, two verbalized); and 2) reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge (RF-LLMaaJ), evaluated in 4-, 8-, and 16-shot settings. For CRPS, lower is better.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on prior work (Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 LLM-as-a-Judge &amp; Reward Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>), we define a Reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge (RF-LLMaaJ) baseline for continuous-score performance prediction using in-context learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vacareanu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. It is prompted with: 1) a brief description of the task and the scoring metric;
2) <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> demonstration examples in form (input, output, gold metric score) of the desired scoring format;
3) instructions to produce a point score and 95% upper/lower interval bound;
and
4) A test pair (input, output) for which it must predict a point estimate and prediction interval for the target metric.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Benchmark</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets &amp; Tasks.</span>
Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.3 Additional Dataset Information ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> details our datasets, tasks, and evaluation metrics used. Tasks span code generation, fact-checking, function-calling, machine translation (MT), question-answering (QA), text summarization, and others. We randomly sample up to 5,000 instances per dataset, splitting each 3/1/1 into train, dev, and test sets. For each question, the LLM generates three outputs. Each output is evaluated based on task-specific metrics, producing a ⟨input, LLM output, metric score⟩ tuple.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Specific Evaluation Metrics.</span> Each task requires predicting one or more continuous metrics. Given our metric-agnostic formulation, the choice of metrics used is less important than assessing the ability to predict arbitrary metrics, including different metrics for the same generation task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Metrics include: BERTScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang* et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, CodeBLEU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and
reference-based (RB) LLM-as-a-judge metrics, such as LLM-Eval (accuracy and informativeness) for QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin and Chen, 2023</a>)</cite> and BiGGen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, GEMBA_ref (with references) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kocmi and Federmann, 2023</a>)</cite> for MT, G-Eval (coherence, consistency, fluency, and relevancy) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for text summarization, FActscore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for fact-checking, and CodeJudge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong and Zhang (2024)</a></cite> for code generation and function-calling (functional correctness and inconsistency). These LLM judge methods support more robust evaluation by considering diverse aspects of quality (e.g., factual correctness, informativeness, fluency, relevance, etc.).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also include a “direct assessment” (DA) task (predicting human-annotated quality ratings instead of automatic metric scores, see Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4 Quality and Metric Estimation in MT ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>), for the BiGGen-Bench-Results dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Because this dataset provides human evaluations for its pre-generated LLM outputs, we use both these annotations and outputs for this DA task.
However, because the dataset only contain one output per rating, this precludes evaluation of consistency-based methods (see Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Confidence Estimation (CE) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs.</span> Llama 3.2 11B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta, 2024</a>)</cite> is used in both generating outputs and in our baseline predictors (i.e., for consistency and verbalized confidence methods, as well as our RF-LLMaaJ). We set <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">max tokens</span> <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=769" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>769</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=769</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">temperature</span> <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">topP</span> <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>. To avoid risk of self-enhancement bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite> in evaluation, we use a different LLM, Gemini-2.0-Flash-Lite, for reference-based (RB) LLM-as-a-judge evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof-of-concept direct assessment evaluation setting differs by using human quality ratings and existing LLM outputs: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite>’s GPT-4-0125 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>)</cite> generations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
Consistency methods are implemented by <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LM-Polygraph</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fadeeva et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and computed over <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!3</annotation></semantics></math> outputs, with all <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> outputs sharing the same consistency score. We implement regression models in <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">scikit-learn</span> and use XGBoost with default hyperparameters. Beta regression is implemented in-house.
For regression model selection, we compare RMSE and CRPS results on the dev set. Based on this (Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we select Random Forest for our test set evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding scale of supervision, for RF-LLMaaJ in-context learning, we evaluate varying <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{4,8,16\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{4,8,16\}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstration examples. We only provide up to 16 examples to the RF-LLMaaJ prompt due to high token costs (especially with long-form outputs), and potentially even degrade performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Modarressi et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In contrast, CE-R is trivial to train at scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">As for relative LLM compute costs of different methods (beyond the output generation call): consistency methods require <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> additional calls (to generate additional outputs for measuring output consistency), verbalized 1S requires no additional calls, verbalized 2S requires one additional call, and RF-LLMaaJ also requires one additional call. In practice, the <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> outputs generated for consistency would be used to select the output predicted to perform best, so the added compute cost is offset by expected quality improvement in output selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Sample Prompts ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> lists prompts and parsing rules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Evaluating Performance Prediction</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t,k}</annotation></semantics></math> (Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 Task Formulation ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) must return both a point estimate <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and a predictive distribution (from which we take a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> interval), and we evaluate these separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Point estimates.</span> We report RMSE between prediction <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and gold score <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, providing an interpretable measure of average error magnitude in the target’s original units and serving as our primary evaluation metric.
Pearson Correlation (PC) is shown only as a diagnostic for feature usefulness (Appendix <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>); it does not directly reflect absolute error, and correlation may be high while still incurring large systematic errors. However, as it is unscaled, it is useful for comparing between tasks and metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction intervals.</span> Expected Calibration Error (ECE) is popular for classification, measuring how well predicted probabilities match observed frequencies via binning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naeini et al., 2015</a>)</cite>. However, ECE is designed for binary scores, not for continuous ones. Prediction intervals for continuous scores can be visualized in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Task Formulation ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, showing each instance’s true score on the y-axis as well as the prediction regressor on the x-axis. Ideally, predictions are not too narrow (overconfident) or too wide (underconfident), and several metrics exist for assessing such miscalibrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Continuous Ranked Probability Score (CRPS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gneiting and Raftery, 2007</a>)</cite> as our primary metric for evaluating prediction intervals. It is a proper scoring rule that jointly rewards calibration and sharpness of the full predictive CDF <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, where lower CRPS is better:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{CRPS}(F,s)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\big(F(x)-\mathbf{1}\{x\geq q\}\big)^{2}\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CRPS</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CRPS}(F,s)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\big(F(x)-\mathbf{1}\{x\geq q\}\big)^{2}\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For interpretability, we also measure Average Coverage Error (ACE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shrivastava et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which compares empirical coverage of the nominal <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> interval to its target as <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ACE}=\bigl|\mathrm{PICP}(p)-p\bigr|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ACE</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>PICP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ACE}=\bigl|\mathrm{PICP}(p)-p\bigr|</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{PICP}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PICP</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{PICP}(p)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\{\,\ell_{i}(p)\leq s_{i}\leq u_{i}(p)\,\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\{\,\ell_{i}(p)\leq s_{i}\leq u_{i}(p)\,\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (we use <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>). Lower ACE indicates better calibration, <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the prediction is under-confident, and <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates it is over-confident. Note that one disadvantage of ACE is that it does not penalize excessively wide intervals (e.g., under-confident intervals that always cover <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> incur a maximum <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> penalty when <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>) as much as narrow ones. Hence, we treat ACE as a secondary, human-readable diagnostic and rely on CRPS for model selection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we address three central questions: 1) Is instance-level performance prediction of continuous, reference-based metrics for long-form LLM outputs feasible? 2) How much supervision is necessary to achieve reasonable performance? and 3) How do confidence-based regression and reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) compare?
We evaluate all baselines across 11 datasets and multiple metrics (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Benchmark ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) using RMSE to judge point estimate accuracy and CRPS to judge calibration of predictive distributions, with ACE and PC used for additional diagnostics (Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Evaluating Performance Prediction ‣ 5 Benchmark ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>). Our key findings are as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance prediction is indeed feasible</span>: regression on off-the-shelf confidence estimates achieves an average RMSE of 0.14 and CRPS of 0.08, with intervals calibrated to within 16% of nominal coverage and requiring as few as 16 training examples to converge.
Tables <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Confidence-Based Regression (CE-Reg) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Confidence-Based Regression (CE-Reg) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> show that regression on the consistency-based “DegMat” confidence estimate performs best in terms of both RMSE and CRPS.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Only 16 training instances are required for the CE-Reg’s test loss to stabilize.</span>
As long-form gold references can be expensive to collect, it is important to understand how sample-efficient these performance prediction methods would be. To answer that, we vary the size of the training set from as few as 4 instances and doubling up to the full training data size for CE-Reg. For RF-LLMaaJ we limit up to 16 instances.
CE-Reg achieves close to our best observed accuracy with remarkably few labeled examples: Figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6 Results ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that RMSE converges after just 16 training instances across all datasets. Even when matched to RF-LLMaaJ’s maximum of 16 in-context examples (Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6 Results ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), CE-Reg delivers substantially lower error.
Although performance varies slightly across datasets, the convergence point is consistent, demonstrating that this regression approach to performance prediction can be effectively trained with a modest sample size, making it easy to collect training data even for out-of-domain tasks and mitigating potential domain differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CE-Reg outperforms RF-LLMaaJ.</span>
In Tables <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Confidence-Based Regression (CE-Reg) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Confidence-Based Regression (CE-Reg) ‣ 4 Baseline Methods ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, CE-Reg methods achieve RMSEs averaging in the 0.14–0.15 range and reaching 0.35 at worst, depending on the task metric. Meanwhile, RF-LLMaaJ incurs much larger errors (averaging around 0.29, 0.85 at worst). Similarly, CE-Reg attains CRPS values between 0.07–0.12 averaged over task metrics, compared to about 0.21 for RF-LLMaaJ.
Interestingly, most methods exhibit an over-confidence (intervals are too narrow) in ACE (95%) (Appendix <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), with verbalized CE-Reg producing ACEs of –0.89 and –0.79 and RF-LLMaaJ yielding ACEs of –0.51 and –0.52, while only some of the consistency-based features come close to nominal coverage (e.g. DegMat ACE: –0.16). Notably, this pattern largely reflects our use of RandomForest regression: substituting a XGBoost model markedly improves ACE, albeit at the expense of other performance metrics (see Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in Appendix).
In addition, Judge scores and human ratings in the BiGGen-Bench-Results dataset yield similar performance, indicating our baselines generalize beyond evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction error is primarily metric-dependent.</span> Across most tasks and methods, CodeBLEU and BERTScore are the easiest metrics to predict: CE-Reg consistently achieves low RMSE and CRPS and high PC on these metrics, and RF-LLMaaJ exhibits a similar trend on most datasets. The G-Eval in text summarization tasks are consistently low in RMSE and CRPS but also low in PC – this can be explained by the fact that its target score distribution is tightly concentrated.
CodeJudge and LLMEvals tend to be the most difficult with high RMSE and CRPS and relatively low PC.
Variation in RMSE and CRPS across evaluation metrics shows that prediction difficulty is largely metric dependent, with low metric-to-metric variability within each task.
In our findings, we do not observe a similar trend for task-to-task variability. Therefore, we do not conclude anything about relative inherent difficulty between tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph-based consistency features consistently outperform all other CE-Reg methods across every dataset and metric</span> Among all CE-Reg, the four graph-based consistency measures (DegMat, Eccentricity, EigVal, LexSim) achieve the lowest CRPS, indicating that capturing variability across multiple generations provides a stronger predictive signal than one- or two-step verbalized confidence prompts, on all types of dataset or metrics. By contrast, the NumSemSets consistency metric performs on par with the verbalized methods, trailing the graph-based techniques. Better results from graph-based consistency over NumSemSets and verbalized confidence are more obviously reflected in the ACE and PC measures (Tables <a href="#A1.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RF-LLMaaJ is a poor performance predictor.</span> Both point prediction and interval estimation are ill-calibrated compared to CE-Reg methods. In fact, only on a few tasks does RF-LLMaaJ seem to have any ability to predict evaluation metrics at all – Appendix Table <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.4 Complementary Tables and Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Instance-level Performance Prediction for Long-form Generation Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows near-zero correlation between predicted and actual scores for the majority of task metrics. Furthermore, increasing in-context examples from 4 to 16 has negligible benefit. There are some task metrics where RF-LLMaaJ significantly better than random chance, but the variability across tasks and metrics highlights how sensitive and unreliable this approach can be, relative to the consistent performance seen in the consistency-based confidence regression approach.
One reason that RF-LLMaaJ struggles to predict evaluation metrics could be its sensitivity to the prompt. A key insight is that, despite both using in-prompt LLM predictions, verbalized confidence is overall more reliable than RF-LLMaaJ, which is prompted to predict arbitrary task metrics. This may reflect the LLM’s pre-training data, which likely contains the general notion of confidence much more so than task-specific metrics like CodeBLEU or GEMBA; and the few-shot examples are not enough to overcome this deficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S6.T3.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T3.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;" rowspan="2"></td>
<td id="S6.T3.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">CE-Reg: Verbalized</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S6.T3.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">RF-LLMaaJ</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">2S</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">4-shot</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">8-shot</span></td>
<td id="S6.T3.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.8pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T3.3.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">16-shot</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
