</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work introduces the task of <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instance-level performance prediction</em> for long-form LLM generation, in which a black-box model’s input–output pair is used to predict arbitrary continuous, reference-based metric scores with associated prediction intervals. To support progress, we release a comprehensive benchmark spanning 11 datasets across summarization, machine translation, code generation, and question-answering, with multiple task evaluation metrics to predict.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments compare two families of performance prediction approaches as baselines. The first–confidence-based regression–extracts off-the-shelf verbalized and consistency-based confidence signals, then trains classic regression models to output both point estimates and prediction intervals. The second–a reference-free LLM-as-a-Judge–asks an LLM, via in-context prompting, to yield metric estimates and 95% intervals for each input-output-instance. We find that confidence-based regression, particularly when using consistency features, yields substantially better RMSE and CRPS than our implemented LLM-based judging. This demonstrates that lightweight supervised regression of simple confidence estimates can outperform few-shot LLM judging for both accuracy and uncertainty quantification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work lays the groundwork for research in uncertainty-aware, per-instance continuous-scored performance prediction. We hope our benchmark, code, and baselines will encourage community efforts toward robust, uncertainty-aware evaluation and deployment of long-form LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
